  "id": "COMPOUND:18771 - PROTEIN:3960",
  "verbalization": "Repenin B is shown to have a target relationship with ATP-dependent dethiobiotin synthetase BioD, which is in turn targeted by multiple compounds, including (3R,4R)-4-methyl-5-oxooxolane-3-carboxylic acid and (9S,13S,16S,18S)-16-hydroxy-5',7,9,13-tetramethylspiro[5-oxapentacyclo[10.8.0.02,9.04,8.013,18]icosane-6,2'-oxane]-10-one. These compounds are all implicated in the regulation of MIO-dependent tyrosine 2,3-aminomutase, a key enzyme in the biosynthesis of aromatic amino acids. The shared pathway from Repenin B to BioD and then to the target enzyme suggests a potential mechanism of action involving the inhibition of dethiobiotin synthetase, which may modulate the activity of the tyrosine 2,3-aminomutase. The evidence supports a direct interaction between Repenin B and the enzyme, with the observed scores indicating a strong and consistent relationship. The mechanism is further supported by the presence of multiple compounds that target the same enzyme, suggesting a possible role in the regulation of the enzyme's activity. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Repenin B to exert its effects through the modulation of dethiobiotin synthetase, which in turn influences the activity of MIO-dependent tyrosine 2,3-aminomutase.",
